  "title": "ObjectLevelConditionalReadWrite",
  "description": "The conditional written on the object whose own properties carry the keyword -- which is the plainest way to spell it and the one no reading of a Go field can see. A property named only by a `then`, an `else`, a `dependentSchemas` branch or a `not` is named by no schema that applies to every valid instance, so the generator builds no field for it and it arrives in the overflow map; the flat key lists are built by walking the fields, so they never ask about it, and mergedPropertyOrigins is written by the allOf and variant merges, which a conditional written directly on the object goes through at all. strict_conditional_read_write.json puts each applicator on a *property's* schema instead, where the value is held as raw JSON and a path table answers -- a real position, but a different one, and passing there says nothing about here. `declaredThenObj` is the other half: the property is declared at object level so it does become a field, and only the annotation is stated in the branch. The root itself carries an if/then for the same reason, because a root schema is the one object that is not reached through a property. Every position carries the same three members -- one writeOnly, one readOnly, one the schema constrains -- so a position that regresses names itself. writeOnly is stripped at all of them; readOnly is refused at none of them, and is refused at `allOfObj` and `inlineObj`, which is what tells the decision apart from the flag being off.",
